DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     A number of supported system architectures allow the behaviour of the CPU
     cache to be programmed to behave differently depending on the region be‐
     ing written.

     The mmeemmccoonnttrrooll utility provides an interface to this facility, allowing
     CPU cache behavior to be altered for ranges of system physical memory.

     These ranges are typically power-of-2 aligned and sized, however the spe‐
     cific rules governing their layout vary between architectures.  The
     mmeemmccoonnttrrooll utility does not attempt to enforce these rules, however the
     system will reject any attempt to set an illegal combination.

     lliisstt   List range slots.

            --aa      List all range slots, even those that are inactive.

     sseett    Set memory range attributes.

            --bb _b_a_s_e
                    Memory range base address.

            --ll _l_e_n_g_t_h
                    Length of memory range in bytes, power of 2.

            --oo _o_w_n_e_r
                    Text identifier for this setting (7 char max).

            _a_t_t_r_i_b_u_t_e
                    Attributes applied to this range; combinations of ffoorrccee,
                    uunnccaacchheeaabbllee, wwrriittee--ccoommbbiinnee, wwrriittee--tthhrroouugghh, wwrriittee--bbaacckk, and
                    wwrriittee--pprrootteecctt.

     cclleeaarr  Clear memory range attributes.  Ranges may be cleared by owner or
            by base/length combination.

            To clear based on ownership:

            --oo _o_w_n_e_r
                    All ranges with this owner will be cleared.

            To clear based on the base/length combination:

            --bb _b_a_s_e
                    Memory range base address.

            --ll _l_e_n_g_t_h
                    Length of memory range in bytes, power of 2.

            Base and length must exactly match an existing range.

